","html_url":"https://github.com/saransh2405/bi-gram-word-suggestion","stars":1,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"In the fields of computational linguistics and probability, an n-gram is a contiguous sequence of n items from a given sequence of text or speech. The items can be phonemes, syllables, letters, words or base pairs according to the application. The n-grams typically are collected from a text or speech corpus. When the items are words, n-grams may also be called shingles.[1]  An n-gram of size 1 is referred to as a \"unigram\"; size 2 is a \"bigram\" (or, less commonly, a \"digram\"); size 3 is a \"trigram\". Larger sizes are sometimes referred to by the value of n, e.g., \"four-gram\", \"five-gram\", and so on.","topics":[],"score":214397,"global_rank":248637,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38713},{"name":"Yukiyukiyeah/Text2VR","owner":"Yukiyukiyeah","html_url":"https://github.com/Yukiyukiyeah/Text2VR","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Attempt to extract components from text to build my VR environment","topics":[],"score":214391,"global_rank":248656,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38714},{"name":"jnhoang/notes","owner":"jnhoang","html_url":"https://github.com/jnhoang/notes","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":0,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":214391,"global_rank":248659,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38715},{"name":"MarcCharbo/stream","owner":"MarcCharbo","html_url":"https://github.com/MarcCharbo/stream","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":null,"topics":[],"score":214387,"global_rank":248668,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":38716},{"name":"lakshadvani/java_work","owner":"lakshadvani","html_url":"https://github.com/lakshadvani/java_work","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"java work","topics":[],"score"